title: 'pallet-revive: runtime-benchmarks no longer changes the events of eth transactions'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    With the `runtime-benchmarks` feature enabled, a successful ethereum transaction no longer
    deposits an `EthExtrinsicRevert` event. Events are now the same with and without the feature.

    `eth_call`, `eth_instantiate_with_code` and `eth_substrate_call` now charge
    `WeightInfo::deposit_eth_extrinsic_revert_event` in their weight annotation and refund it when
    the transaction succeeds. A failed transaction keeps the charge for the `EthExtrinsicRevert`
    event it deposits.

    The `eth_call`, `eth_instantiate_with_code` and `eth_substrate_call` benchmarks no longer
    include depositing that event. Runtimes that use their own `pallet_revive` weights should
    re-run these benchmarks. Until then, the old weights charge the event twice on a failed
    transaction and once on a successful one, which overcharges but is safe. No other migration
    is needed.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: patch
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: none
